/**
@mainpage USBRadioPlus developer reference

USBRadioPlus is a Rust radio driver with a Rust Asterisk host and a
metadata-only C module entry point.
Review the accepted architecture and ADRs in the rpt_advanced repository before
changing a component boundary.

@section source_layout Source layout

- `rust/core` parses and resolves configuration.
- `rust/radio`, `rust/runtime`, and `rust/station` own radio signaling, media,
  processing, and station composition.
- `rust/audio`, `rust/gpio`, `rust/ring`, `rust/samplerate`, `rust/ffmpeg`, and
  `rust/rnnoise` validate and own the released provider descriptors.
- `rust/driver` owns configured stations and their lifecycle.
- `rust/asl3` owns the Asterisk-compatible 8 kHz and rate-aware 48 kHz media
  adapters.
- `rust/asterisk` owns Asterisk channel hosting, delivery, link audiohooks,
  reload, CLI commands, and module lifecycle behind a versioned loader table.
- `rust/tune` builds the installed `usbradioplus-tune` utility.
- `rust/rms-agc` builds the private LADSPA gain-rider plugin used by FFmpeg.
- `src/chan_usbradioplus_shim.c` is the only production C source. It composes
  provider metadata, forwards load, reload, and unload to Rust, and declares
  the Asterisk module metadata.

@section boundary Asterisk boundary

`RadioPlus` exchanges fixed 8 kHz signed-linear Asterisk frames.
Unattached `RadioPlusAdvanced` exchanges 48 kHz signed-linear frames. Rust owns all
configuration, audio processing, radio hardware, control semantics, and station
lifecycle, plus all Asterisk host behavior. The C entry point must not duplicate
those decisions.

The driver descriptor must outlive every channel. A reserved channel is started
once, stopped before destruction, and destroyed before the driver. Voice writes
are the sole producer for the Rust program handoff. The channel taskprocessor is
the sole owner of all other Rust channel operations. The delivery worker queues
voice, text, and control frames back to Asterisk without holding a Rust audio
lock. Hangup clears ownership and joins the worker before destroying the Rust
channel.

The direct native POC attaches `struct urp_ast_direct_callbacks` from
`rust/asterisk/include/usbradioplus_asterisk.h` using public
`ast_channel_setoption(channel, URP_AST_OPTION_DIRECT_CALLBACKS, &callbacks,
sizeof(callbacks), 0)` before `call()`. Only `RadioPlusAdvanced` accepts this
private initial-alpha option (RPAD, `0x52504144`); exact descriptor size, version
two, both contexts and both functions are required. The caller provides a writable
descriptor with `accepted_abi_version = 0` and checks both the return status and
acknowledgment before starting the channel. The host writes version two only after
successful retention. Revision-one controllers must be rebuilt with this contract;
the paired USBRadioPlus and rpt_advanced artifacts must be installed together.
Repeated or late attachment
fails. The taskprocessor copies the descriptor; its owner retains both contexts
and their executable code until synchronous stop/hangup quiesces PortAudio, and
must keep them valid if the channel will restart.

Attached channels start no Asterisk delivery worker. USB receive DSP runs first,
publishes hardware observations and latest receive qualification, then calls the
direct receive endpoint with mutable normalized mono F32. The transmit endpoint
fills preallocated mono scratch; those exact samples bypass the rate-adjusting
program ring and feed the same radio render. Its key result is ORed with local
PTT before rendering. These native callbacks must not allocate, lock, block, log,
call Asterisk, perform filesystem/network I/O or change reference counts. Reload
copies the same attachment into the replacement before opening/starting it and
stops the previous PortAudio stream first. `RadioPlus` and unattached Advanced
retain their existing controller, ring and delivery paths. The private internal
Rust host descriptor is version four; the loader ABI remains four. This POC
changes no released shared-provider SONAME or external provider ABI.

A failed direct TX callback or invalid key immediately publishes hardware unkey,
stages silence, and advances the radio with external PTT/test-tone requests
disabled. Returned signaling-tail audio/PTT is suppressed for that failed block.
The preallocated staged source has separate shared ownership and interior
mutability; staging never reborrows the consumer retained by the radio port.
Preparation, staging and rendering are serialized by the sole TX owner, with no
overlapping source access or callback reference-count changes.

A reload is one serialized transaction. Rust stages a private configuration;
every live channel preflights its hardware and builds its media generation, and
every attached link builds a replacement graph before publication. Eligible
links discovered during staging receive a fully attached dormant hook before
publication, so commit only swaps prepared pointers. Reload entries retain the
hook itself rather than an Asterisk channel, allowing a channel masquerade
during staging. The Rust host then adopts every channel, publishes the driver
generation, swaps link graphs in place, and retires rollback ownership.
Request, hangup, link-scan membership, and ordinary taskprocessor admission are
frozen for that interval. Transmit key/unkey indications remain nonblocking:
the latest intent is replayed after the transaction, so an unkey cannot be
lost. Any fallible-phase error restores the old driver, channels, and link
processing; a physical restart failure is logged and leaves the affected
station RF-safe and stopped. Removing a live channel is rejected. Jitter
queries and ordinary service never trigger a lazy refresh.

@section providers Dynamic providers

The loader passes the installed descriptors for the rate-adjusting PCM ring,
radio core, sample-rate adapter, FFmpeg adapter, RNNoise adapter,
PortAudio/ALSA adapter, and GPIO adapter to Rust. The module and Rust adapter
link to their released, versioned shared objects. No provider implementation is
vendored or statically linked.

@section realtime Real-time ownership

Provider audio callbacks and Rust station processing are allocation-free and
lock-free after setup. Asterisk frame allocation and queueing remain outside
the hardware callback. Control work runs on the channel taskprocessor. Status
publication uses snapshots rather than ownership of live DSP state.

Incoming ASL3 link channels are the one remaining Asterisk audiohook boundary.
A control-plane scanner prepares each negotiated-rate Rust graph and its
bounded workspaces before attachment. The callback processes read audio in
place without allocation, locking, logging, or queued latency. Reload prepares
each candidate outside the callback and swaps the Rust handle under Asterisk's
existing audiohook quiescence; the hook is not detached. A direct hook
reference keeps staged ownership valid if Asterisk moves its datastore during
a masquerade. Invalid reloads leave the current generation attached; a dormant
hook created for a new link remains a clean bypass until the scanner can
prepare the restored generation.

@section verification Verification

`make lint static-analysis` runs the platform-independent checks. `make check`
builds the module and Rust artifacts, runs the Rust workspace and the frozen RMS
AGC parity test, and validates release artifacts. `make docs` requires warning-
free Rustdoc and Doxygen output. `make platform-verify` adds production coverage,
real-module startup smoke testing, a deterministic source archive, and staged
installation. The pull-request workflow runs the complete Debian 13 native
platform gate.
*/
